WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.860
I'm absolutely here for the&nbsp;
Grimace-shakes-will-kill you-trend but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.420
So. Many. Questions. Starting with; Is Grimace&nbsp;
a monster? Yes, Grimace is most definitely a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.040
monster. When he was introduced in 1971, he&nbsp;
had four arms looked kind of scaly and stole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.480
milkshakes from children - which is terrifying&nbsp;
and rude. McDonald's realized pretty quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.620
driving kids away was not good for business and&nbsp;
Grimace was made over into the amorphous purple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.240
blob we know and love today. Grimace lore says&nbsp;
he might be shaped to look like a taste bud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.920
weird, though the official stand is that he's the&nbsp;
embodiment of a milkshake. Which is just as weird.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.320
Could this original evil Grimace incarnation be&nbsp;
behind the deadly milkshake trend? The best I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.100
figure is that Grimace is a byproduct of the&nbsp;
1970s and some corporate branding practices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.580
and this month the internet made him&nbsp;
their own by injecting some horror back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.480
into Ronald's best friend. Another win for&nbsp;
modern pop culture monsters and capitalism.

